\b Appendix A: Screen Reader Testing Notes\b0\par
\par
\b Project Information\b0\par
Application: EduLense Desktop\par
Repository: umgc/2026_spring\par
Branch: developer\par
Local project directory: /Users/kwameduodu/Edulense/EduLense/edulence_desktop\par
User interface stack: Electron + React\par
\par
\b Purpose\b0\par
This appendix documents manual and code-assisted screen reader verification performed for the EduLense Desktop application. The goal of this testing was to confirm that core navigation, page announcements, dialogs, and primary interactive controls are usable with assistive technologies and keyboard-only workflows.\par
\par
\b Test Environment\b0\par
\bullet macOS with VoiceOver\par
\bullet Keyboard-only navigation during all checks\par
\bullet Jest + React Testing Library for automated keyboard navigation verification\par
\par
Windows NVDA verification is recommended as a follow-up validation activity when a Windows desktop environment is available.\par
\par
\b Scope\b0\par
The following areas of the application were included in the screen reader review:\par
\bullet Authentication screen\par
\bullet Main sidebar navigation\par
\bullet Top toolbar controls\par
\bullet Route heading announcements after navigation\par
\bullet Keyboard shortcuts dialog\par
\bullet Notes editor\par
\bullet Settings controls\par
\bullet Structural landmarks and active route state\par
\par
\b Accessibility Behaviors Verified\b0\par
\b 1. Authentication Screen\b0\par
\bullet The welcome heading is focusable and can be announced when the application loads in an unauthenticated state.\par
\bullet Primary authentication actions are exposed as standard buttons.\par
\bullet Keyboard users can reach and activate Sign In and Create Account without pointer interaction.\par
\par
\b 2. Main Navigation\b0\par
\bullet The desktop shell exposes a navigation landmark for the sidebar.\par
\bullet Sidebar destinations are reachable using Tab.\par
\bullet Focus is visible while moving through navigation controls.\par
\bullet Sidebar activation works with keyboard input, including Enter.\par
\bullet The active route is programmatically exposed with aria-current="page" on the current navigation item.\par
\par
\b 3. Route Announcement Behavior\b0\par
\bullet After route changes, the primary page heading receives focus.\par
\bullet This improves screen reader announcement reliability for the active page.\par
\bullet Verified for core destinations including Dashboard, Courses, Notes, and Settings.\par
\par
\b 4. Keyboard Shortcuts Dialog\b0\par
\bullet The shortcuts overlay is announced as a modal dialog.\par
\bullet The dialog exposes a meaningful accessible name: Keyboard Shortcuts.\par
\bullet The close control is keyboard reachable and screen-reader discoverable.\par
\bullet The dialog can be dismissed without pointer interaction.\par
\par
\b 5. Notes Editor\b0\par
\bullet The notes editor textarea is exposed with the accessible name Note content.\par
\bullet Editor toolbar buttons are labeled and keyboard reachable.\par
\bullet Note list navigation and note selection controls are exposed as interactive elements.\par
\par
\b 6. Settings Controls\b0\par
\bullet Theme controls expose radio semantics.\par
\bullet Preference toggles use checkbox inputs and are announced with associated labels.\par
\bullet Users can identify both the control name and state when navigating with a screen reader.\par
\par
\b Automated Verification\b0\par
Automated keyboard-navigation tests were added to support the manual screen reader checks. These tests verify the keyboard behavior that screen reader users typically depend on.\par
\par
Verified automated behaviors:\par
\bullet Tab moves focus between sidebar destinations\par
\bullet Enter activates the focused sidebar route\par
\bullet Ctrl/Cmd+2 navigates to Courses\par
\bullet Active route semantics update after keyboard navigation\par
\par
Test file references:\par
\bullet renderer/src/__tests__/App.test.jsx\par
\bullet renderer/src/__tests__/App.coverage.test.jsx\par
\par
Recommended verification command:\par
\f1
cd /Users/kwameduodu/Edulense/EduLense/edulence_desktop\line
npx jest --runInBand renderer/src/__tests__/App.test.jsx renderer/src/__tests__/App.coverage.test.jsx\par
\f0
\par
\b Manual Screen Reader Test Procedure\b0\par
1. Launch the application.\par
2. Confirm that the authentication heading is announced in the unauthenticated state.\par
3. Use keyboard-only navigation to reach and activate the Sign In button.\par
4. Move into the main sidebar using Tab.\par
5. Navigate between Dashboard, Courses, Notes, and Settings.\par
6. Activate destinations with Enter.\par
7. Confirm the newly loaded page heading is announced after each route change.\par
8. Open the shortcuts dialog using Cmd/Ctrl+/.\par
9. Confirm the dialog is announced as a modal and that its close button is reachable.\par
10. Navigate to Settings and verify radio buttons and checkboxes announce both label and state.\par
11. Navigate to Notes and confirm the editor is announced as Note content.\par
\par
\b Results Summary\b0\par
The screen reader review found that the current desktop prototype supports the primary accessibility behaviors needed for keyboard and assistive technology navigation in the core shell experience.\par
\par
Confirmed strengths:\par
\bullet Landmark-based navigation is present.\par
\bullet Active route semantics are exposed.\par
\bullet Route headings are focus-managed for announcement.\par
\bullet Dialogs are labeled and keyboard accessible.\par
\bullet Form controls in settings are readable and operable.\par
\bullet Keyboard-only navigation works across primary application areas.\par
\par
\b Known Limitations\b0\par
\bullet Several destinations are placeholder views, so accessibility validation is strongest in the shared shell and implemented pages rather than in fully developed feature workflows.\par
\bullet Automated tests validate keyboard behavior, but they do not replace manual screen reader testing with VoiceOver and NVDA.\par
\bullet Additional Windows-specific validation is still recommended before formal release.\par
\par
\b Conclusion\b0\par
EduLense Desktop demonstrates acceptable screen reader support for its current scope. Core navigation, route announcements, dialogs, settings controls, and note editing entry points are usable with keyboard-only interaction and assistive technology workflows. The addition of active-route semantics and explicit keyboard navigation verification significantly improves confidence in desktop accessibility behavior.\par
}
